2026-09-13|Maximum Overlap of Two Binary Images

Question
- Given two binary matrices img1 and img2 of size n × n, return the maximum number of overlapping 1s after translating one image in any direction.

Approach
1. Store the coordinates of all cells containing 1 in img1 and img2.

2. Suppose a 1 at (r1, c1) in img1 overlaps a 1 at (r2, c2) in img2.

3. The required translation is:
   dr = r2 - r1
   dc = c2 - c1

4. Every pair of 1s produces one possible translation.

5. Use a hash map to count how many pairs of 1s produce the same `(dr, dc)` translation.

6. If a particular translation occurs many times, it means many 1s from img1 overlap 1s from img2 after applying that translation.

7. The maximum frequency among all translations is therefore the maximum possible overlap.

8. This approach automatically handles:
- Horizontal shifts.
- Vertical shifts.
- Diagonal shifts.
- Bits moving outside the matrix, because only coordinates that correspond to existing 1s in both images are counted.

Solution

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    int largestOverlap(vector<vector<int>>& img1,
                       vector<vector<int>>& img2) {
        int n = img1.size();

        vector<pair<int, int>> ones1;
        vector<pair<int, int>> ones2;

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {
                if (img1[i][j] == 1)
                    ones1.push_back({i, j});

                if (img2[i][j] == 1)
                    ones2.push_back({i, j});
            }
        }

        map<pair<int, int>, int> freq;

        int ans = 0;

        for (auto [r1, c1] : ones1) {
            for (auto [r2, c2] : ones2) {
                int dr = r2 - r1;
                int dc = c2 - c1;

                freq[{dr, dc}]++;

                ans = max(ans, freq[{dr, dc}]);
            }
        }

        return ans;
    }
};
```

- Example:
- Suppose three pairs of 1s produce the same translation `(2, -1)`.
- That means after shifting img1 by 2 rows downward and 1 column left, those three pairs overlap.
- Therefore, that translation gives an overlap of 3.

- The important idea is to count translations rather than trying every shifted matrix explicitly.

- Time Complexity: O(k₁ × k₂), where k₁ and k₂ are the numbers of 1s in img1 and img2.
- In the worst case, k₁ = k₂ = n², giving O(n⁴).
- Space Complexity: O(k₁ × k₂) in the worst case for the translation frequency map.
